Two things to know before using it

Weights. The sample is not self-weighting. Unweighted counts describe the sample, not the population. For any population estimate, weight by weight_state (or weight_district for district-level work).

Duplicates. Fifteen person records appear twice in the source Block 2 file. They are left in as-is rather than silently dropped. Deduplicate with df.drop_duplicates("person_id") if your analysis needs unique persons.

The time diary

build_activity_dataset.py adds Block 3, the diary itself. Run it after build_person_dataset.py. It streams the 3.17 million activity records in chunks, so it runs on a normal laptop.

It writes three files:

activity_episodes_TUS_1998.csv.gz — the granular file. One row per activity episode, 3,170,534 rows, gzipped to about 20 MB. Columns: person and household ids, sex, age, state, district, sector, day type, hour slot, activity serial, hour from and to, minutes, activity code, activity division, SNA group, whether simultaneous, within or outside the household, mode of payment, and weights. Read it with pd.read_csv(path); pandas handles the gzip automatically.

person_day_division_minutes.csv — 302,622 rows of person by day type by division totals, in long format.

person_activity_summary_TUS_1998.csv — 63,173 rows, one per person, minutes in each of the nine divisions on their normal day, joined to all the demographic and household columns above.

Diary conventions
The diary day runs 4 am to 4 am, in 24 one-hour slots.
Each person was recorded for up to three day types: normal (2,206,337 episodes), weekly variant (884,073) and abnormal (80,124).
Minutes on a normal day sum to exactly 1440 for every person bar one (2956700060102, whose diary is duplicated in the source).
Activities can be simultaneous; the simultaneous_activity flag marks those, and the survey split the minutes between them rather than double-counting, which is why the totals close at 1440.
Activity codes

The three-digit activity code is kept raw in activity_code, because the code descriptions live in the NSS instruction manual and not in these data files. Only the nine major divisions (the first digit) are labelled, in activity_division:

Primary production
Secondary production
Trade, business and services
Household maintenance, management and shopping
Care of children, the sick, elderly and disabled
Community services and help to other households
Learning
Social, cultural and recreational activity
Personal care and self-maintenance

sna_group collapses these further: divisions 1 to 3 fall inside the System of National Accounts production boundary, 4 to 6 are unpaid work outside it, and 7 to 9 are not production at all. This grouping is the basis of most published analysis of the survey. Verify the division labels against the survey manual before publishing anything from them.

Data source

National Sample Survey Organisation, Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation, Government of India. The raw survey files are not stored in this repository.
